al cell lines: expression of cytokines and chemokines.</article-title><source>Neurobiol Dis</source><volume>8</volume><fpage>1057</fpage><lpage>1068</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11741401</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002829-Reiber3"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Reiber</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Peter</surname><given-names>JB</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Cerebrospinal fluid analysis: disease-related data patterns and evaluation programs.</article-title><source>J Neurol Sci</source><volume>184</volume><fpage>101</fpage><lpage>122</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11239944</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002829-Kalaria1"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kalaria</surname><given-names>RN</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>Microglia and Alzheimer's disease.</article-title><source>Curr Opin Hematol</source><volume>6</volume><fpage>15</fpage><lpage>24</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9915549</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002829-Schenk1"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Schenk</surname><given-names>DB</given-names></name><name><surname>Yednock</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>The role of microglia in Alzheimer's disease: friend or foe?</article-title><source>Neurobiol Aging</source><volume>23</volume><fpage>677</fpage><lpage>679; discussion 683&#x02013;674</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12392771</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002829-Zlokovic1"><label>25</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zlokovic</surname><given-names>BV</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Clearing amyloid through the blood-brain barrier.</article-title><source>J Neurochem</source><volume>89</volume><fpage>807</fpage><lpage>811</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15140180</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002829-Golde1"><label>26</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Golde</surname><given-names>TE</given-names></name><name><surname>Dickson</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Hutton</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Filling the gaps in the abeta cascade hypothesis of Alzheimer's disease.</article-title><source>Curr Alzheimer Res</source><volume>3</volume><fpage>421</fpage><lpage>430</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17168641</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002829-Lee2"><label>27</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>HJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>KS</given-names></name><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>EJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Choi</surname><given-names>HB</given-names></name><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>KH</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Brain transplantation of immortalized human neural stem cells promotes functional recovery in mouse intracerebral hemorrhage stroke model.</article-title><source>Stem Cells</source><volume>25</volume><fpage>1204</fpage><lpage>1212</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17218400</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002829-Kim1"><label>28</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>JY</given-names></name><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name><name><surname>Park</surname><given-names>GW</given-names></name><name><surname>Cho</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Kwon</surname><given-names>KH</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Utility of electrophoretically derived protein mass estimates as additional constraints in proteome analysis of human serum based on MS/MS analysis.</article-title><source>Proteomics</source><volume>5</volume><fpage>3376</fpage><lpage>3385</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16052618</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002829-Roepstorff1"><label>29</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Roepstorff</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><year>1994</year><article-title>The interplay between different mass spectrometric techniques in protein chemistry.</article-title><source>Biochem Soc Trans</source><volume>22</volume><fpage>533</fpage><lpage>539</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7958361</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn fn-type="conflict"><p><bold>Competing Interests: </bold>The authors have declared that no competing interests exist.</p></fn><fn fn-type="financial-disclosure"><p><bold>Funding: </bold>This work was supported by a Korea Science and Engineering Foundation grant (#2006-04090), 21ST frontier science program SC-2110 of Korea stem cell research center, and a National Health &#x00026; Medical Research Council Program Grant (#280912, R.J.S).</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>